  "final_text": "I built the dashboard and created the alert rule. The rule has already run once with status \"ok\", which means its query is valid. I didn't open the dashboard to see whether the panels render data, and I didn't test the rule against a real 5xx spike.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** Each record in the \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" data view is one web request, timed by the `timestamp` field. It records the visitor's IP address (`clientip`), the page requested (`url`, `request`), the response code (`response.keyword`, such as \"503\"), the size of the reply (`bytes`), the source and destination countries (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`), the operating system and browser (`machine.os`, `agent`), and the file type requested (`extension`).\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-5b6bcc4f Web logs overview\"**, with 7 panels:\n- **Single numbers:** total requests, unique visitors (counted by IP address), and total bytes served.\n- **Requests over time by response code:** a bar chart showing the top 5 codes.\n- **Response codes:** a pie chart of how requests split across codes.\n- **Top requested URLs:** a table with request counts and bytes served.\n- **Traffic by source country:** a bar chart of the top 10 countries.\n\nOpen it here: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-5b6bcc4f-web-logs-overview-01a54b42c6b8\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-5b6bcc4f 5xx spike\"** (id `a8b6b1e1-f747-4f26-ad3f-0ad30bf51430`):\n- It checks `kibana_sample_data_logs` every minute and looks back over the last 5 minutes.\n- It fires when more than 10 requests in that window have a response code from 500 to 599. Codes are stored as text, so the rule compares them alphabetically; that works because they are all three digits long.\n- I turned it on when creating it. It has no actions attached, so when it fires it only records an alert in Kibana and doesn't send notifications.",
